<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicobar Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nicobar Islands are an archipelago in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, today part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s union territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andaman-and-nicobar-islands">Andaman and Nicobar Islands</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> claimed them from the 18th century and made several failed attempts at settlement before giving up the claim in 1868-1869. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> handed them nominally to Azad Hind.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicobar-islands.jpg" alt="Nicobar Islands" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: edited by M.Minderhoud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicobar_Islands.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Nicobar Islands are an archipelago in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, 150 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and about 1,300 kilometres south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. With the Andaman Islands to the north they mark the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> and the Andaman Sea. They cover 1,841 square kilometres in three groups, northern, central and southern. The largest island is Great Nicobar, at 922 square kilometres, whose southern point, Indira Point, is also the southernmost point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The islands belong to a great arc created by the collision of the Indo-Australian Plate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>, and the climate is warm and tropical, with heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> rain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The islands are believed to have been inhabited for several thousand years. Six indigenous Nicobarese languages belong to the Austroasiatic family, and the Shompen of southern Great Nicobar may be of Mesolithic Southeast Asian origin. The earliest known reference to the name is in the Sri Lankan Pali chronicles, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dipavamsa">Dipavamsa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>, which describe an island of naked people. In the ninth century the Persian explorer Abū Zayd al-Sīrāfī called the islands Lanjabālūs. The modern name probably derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> name Nakkavaram, which appears on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> inscription of 1050 CE.</p><p>Organised European colonisation began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danish-east-india-company">Danish East India Company</a> in the 18th century, when the islands were administered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tharangambadi">Tranquebar</a> as Frederiksøerne. Moravian missionaries who tried to settle on Nancowry died in great numbers from disease, and outbreaks of malaria repeatedly forced the settlers out until the islands were gradually abandoned for good from 1848. Between 1778 and 1783 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-bolts">William Bolts</a> tried to found an Austrian colony, mistakenly assuming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> had given up its claims. Denmark formally ended its presence on 16 October 1868, selling its rights to the United Kingdom, which in 1869 made them part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied the islands between 1942 and 1945, and the British regained them after the Japanese surrender in 1945. Together with the Andaman Islands they became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1950 and a union territory in 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The islands form the Nicobar district of the union territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andaman-and-nicobar-islands">Andaman and Nicobar Islands</a>. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> recorded 36,844 people, among them the Nicobarese and the Shompen. They are recognised as a distinct terrestrial ecoregion with many endemic species, and the Great Nicobar Biosphere Reserve was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s network of biosphere reserves in 2013. On 26 December 2004 a tsunami devastated the coast, killing at least 6,000 people in the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicobar Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
